course: everyday
lesson: 111
title: Chores and broken things
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A home runs well when everyone shares the work: each thing has its own place, jobs are done a little at a time, and they are done properly rather than quickly and badly.
explain: Many chores have a small safety reason inside them: carry a few plates, not a tower; pick up socks and cords before vacuuming; water a plant only when its soil is dry.
explain: When something breaks, the real danger is the broken pieces, not bad luck: tiny glass slivers fly far and cut bare feet, so you stop, keep others away and tell a grown-up.
explain: For example, a boy in Ohio drops a glass: he stands still, tells his little sister and the dog to stay out, puts shoes on and calls his dad before anyone walks through the kitchen.
explain: And if you break something by accident, telling the truth at once and saying sorry is always better than hiding it.
fact: Having a place for each thing and tidying a little every day makes things quick to find and stops people tripping.
fact: Carrying a few plates at a time is safer than carrying a big pile that can slip and break.
fact: Before stacking plates, scrape the leftovers into the bin or the food caddy, a small bin just for food scraps.
fact: Dishes should be dried with a clean dry towel, because dishes put away wet can go mouldy.
fact: Before vacuuming, pick up small things, socks and cords, and keep the vacuum away from hair and pets' tails.
fact: A pet needs the right amount of food at the same time each day and fresh water, because it depends on you.
fact: A houseplant should be watered when its soil feels dry, because too much water rots the roots.
fact: In a family everyone shares the work, so you pause a game to help when you are asked.
fact: Money for chores is earned by doing the job well, not by doing it fast and badly.
fact: After breaking glass, stand still, keep people and pets away, put shoes on and tell a grown-up.
fact: Glass is picked up with a dustpan and brush and a damp paper towel for tiny pieces, never bare fingers, and wrapped in paper before the bin.
fact: A cracked or chipped glass or mug should not be used, because it can break in your hand or leave glass in a drink.
fact: Breaking a mirror does not bring seven years of bad luck; that is a superstition, and the only danger is the glass.
fact: Old glass thermometers may contain mercury, which is poisonous, so a grown-up must clean up a broken one.
exercise: Your room is so messy that you cannot find your homework. What habit helps most? | Keep a place for each thing and tidy a little every day | Tidy everything once a month in one big go | Keep all your things in one big pile on the bed so they are in one place | Ask your teacher for a new copy each time
exercise: Why should you carry only a few plates when clearing the table? | A big pile is hard to hold and can slip and break | Plates get dirty much faster when they are stacked up in a pile | It makes the job take less time | Grown-ups like to count the trips
exercise: Where do leftovers go when you clear the table? | Scraped into the bin or food caddy before the plates are stacked | Left on the plates in the kitchen sink overnight for someone else | Poured down the bathroom sink | Fed to the pet from each plate
exercise: Why should dishes be dry before they are put away? | Wet dishes stacked away can go mouldy | Wet dishes make the cupboards colder inside | Dry dishes weigh less sitting on the shelf | Wet dishes slowly lose their bright colour
exercise: What should you do before vacuuming your bedroom floor? | Pick up small things, socks and cords first | Turn the vacuum cleaner up to its very strongest setting | Open all the windows wide | Move the furniture into the hallway
exercise: Why must a vacuum be kept away from a person's long hair or a cat's tail? | The vacuum can suck them in and tangle them | The noise makes hair fall out | The vacuum stops working properly when it gets near fur | Hair makes the vacuum smell bad
exercise: Feeding the family dog is your chore. What is the right way? | Give the right amount at the same time each day, with fresh water | Give it as much food as it wants, whenever it comes and begs for more | Feed it only when you remember | Give it water only on hot days
exercise: When should you water a houseplant? | When the soil feels dry to the touch | Every hour so it never gets thirsty | Only when its leaves have fallen off | Never, because plants live on air
exercise: Your mum asks for help while you are in the middle of a game. What is best? | Pause the game, help, then go back to it | Finish the whole game first | Pretend you did not hear her | Say it is your brother's turn to help this time, not yours
exercise: You are paid pocket money for washing the car. How should you do it? | Properly, because money is earned by doing a job well | As fast as possible so that you get paid sooner and can go out | Only the parts that people can see | Half of it now and half next month
exercise: Why do we match socks and fold clothes into drawers? | Clothes stay neat, do not crease and are easy to find | Folded clothes get cleaner by themselves | Drawers break and fall apart if the clothes in them are not folded | Unfolded clothes shrink in the drawer
exercise: You break a glass on the kitchen floor. What should you do first? | Stand still, keep others and pets away and tell a grown-up | Run barefoot to fetch a brush | Pick up every piece fast with your fingers before anyone sees | Kick the pieces under the cupboard
exercise: How should tiny pieces of broken glass be picked up? | With a dustpan and brush, then a damp paper towel | With bare fingers, picking them up one by one so none are missed | By blowing them into a corner | With a dry sock on your hand
exercise: Why wrap broken glass in paper before putting it in the bin? | It protects the person who empties the bin | It makes the bin smell much better for everyone | Glass melts if left unwrapped | Bins only take wrapped rubbish
exercise: You broke your mum's favourite vase while playing. What is right? | Tell the truth straight away and say sorry | Hide all the pieces under the sofa and hope | Glue it and say nothing | Blame it on the cat
exercise: You notice a small chip on the rim of your glass. What should you do? | Stop using it and show a grown-up | Drink from the other side of the rim | Use it only for cold drinks from now on | Wash it and put it back in the cupboard
exercise: A mirror falls and breaks. What is the real danger? | The sharp broken glass, not bad luck | Seven whole years of bad luck for the family | The mirror can no longer reflect light | The wall behind it will crack as well
exercise: An old glass thermometer breaks and silver beads roll out. What should you do? | Tell a grown-up and do not touch the beads | Collect the beads in your hand to play with | Vacuum them up quickly | Wash them down the sink
test: Ella in Leeds finds that her mess makes her late every morning. Which change would help her most? | Giving her things a place and tidying a little each day | Buying more school things so some are always lying about | Leaving everything out so she can see it | Tidying only when friends come round
test: Which is true about doing chores for pocket money? | The job should be done well, because that is what earns the money | The faster the job is done, the more it is worth | A job only counts if a grown-up stands and watches you doing it | Doing half the job earns the whole money
test: A boy waters his cactus every day, and it goes soft and brown at the bottom. What went wrong? | Too much water rotted its roots | It needed even more water every day | Cacti hate being looked at too often | It was much too dry in its little pot
test: Sam drops a jar in the kitchen in Sydney while his baby sister crawls nearby. What matters most first? | Keeping his sister and the pets away from the glass | Finding out how much the jar cost | Saving the jam that spilled | Sweeping it all away barefoot before anyone in the house sees the mess
test: Why is a damp paper towel good for the last bits of glass? | Tiny slivers stick to it that a brush misses | It makes the glass soft and safe | It washes the glass down the drain | It makes the slivers bigger and easier to see
test: Lily hid the pieces of a broken bowl in the bin and said nothing. Why was that a bad idea? | It hurts trust, and someone could cut themselves on the hidden pieces | The bowl could have mended itself in the bin if it was left alone overnight | Hidden pieces turn into dust | Bins cannot take broken bowls
test: Your friend says that breaking a mirror means seven years of bad luck. What is the truth? | It is a superstition; only the broken glass is a danger | It is true, but only for small mirrors that break in the bathroom | It is true unless you say sorry | It brings seven weeks, not seven years
test: Why is a chipped mug a risk even if it still holds tea? | It can crack in your hand or leave glass bits in the drink | Chipped mugs make the tea inside go cold much faster than whole ones | The chip makes the tea taste of soap | Chipped mugs cannot be washed
test: Why should cords be picked up before you vacuum? | The vacuum can suck them in, tangle and break | Cords make the vacuum too quiet | Cords stop the floor from getting properly clean underneath | The vacuum is scared of cords
test: smashed a glass in the kitchen, barefoot rn | Stand still, keep others away, get shoes on and tell a grown-up | Pick up the big bits quickly with your fingers | Walk out carefully on tiptoe and leave it for later | Sweep it under the cupboard
test: broke mums mirror, 7 yrs bad luck now?? | No, that's a superstition; just be careful of the glass | Yes, unless you bury the pieces in the garden tonight | Yes, but only for three years | Only if it breaks into seven bits
test: my mug's got a chip on the rim, still ok to use? | No, it can break or leave glass bits in your drink | Yes, just drink from the other side and it's fine | Yes, chips make it stronger | Only for cold drinks
test: plant looks sad, water it every day loads? | Water it when the soil feels dry | Yes, lots of water every single day | Never water it, plants find their own | Only ever water the leaves, not the soil
explain: A boy in Leeds picked up a broken glass with his bare hands; he cut his palm and needed stitches.
explain: A girl in Ohio broke her mum's vase and hid the pieces under her bed; she later cut her foot on a sharp piece she had forgotten.
fact: Broken glass and china pieces are sharp and can be hidden in carpets or under furniture for days.
fact: Telling an adult about something broken means it can be cleaned up safely.
fact: Never mix bleach with other cleaners, because together they can make a poisonous gas.
test: smashed a glass, gonna pick up the bits quick | Stay back, put shoes on and get an adult to clean it | Pick them up barehanded fast before mum sees the mess | Kick them under the sofa | Walk on them to crush them
test: broke mum's mug, gonna hide it | Tell her, hidden pieces can cut someone | Hide it under the bed and say nothing | Glue it and say nothing | Blame the cat
test: chores: can i use bleach and other cleaner together for extra clean | No, mixing cleaners can make poisonous gas | Yes, it cleans twice as well that way | Yes, if you open a window | Only mix a little
test: did u hear, Max smashed a mirror at his nan's, she says 7 yrs bad luck now lol. what's the real lesson | The only danger is the sharp glass, so clear it safely | He really will have seven years of bad luck now | He should touch wood straight away to cancel out all the bad luck | Mirrors are safe to pick up because they are flat
test: apparently a kid at my school dropped a glass n there was glass "in every room of the house" for a month. what's probs exaggerated | Every room for a month, though slivers do fly far | That glass can cut bare feet at all | That tiny pieces can hide in the carpet | That you should put shoes on straight after a glass breaks on the floor
test: Ellie hid the vase she broke n everyone at the bus stop is laughing she's a liar, should i pass it on | No, it is unkind; the lesson is to own up at once | Yes, everyone deserves to know she lied | Yes, but only to her best friends | Yes, as long as you change a few of the details so it is not so obvious
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the conduct required immediately subsequent to the breakage of a drinking glass. | Remain still, keep others away, wear shoes and inform an adult | Gather the larger fragments promptly with the bare hands | Continue walking normally, since the fragments are visible | Vacuum the whole area first and only after that notify an adult of what happened
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a houseplant ought to be watered solely when its soil is dry to the touch. | Excessive watering causes the roots to rot | Dry soil is the plant's sole source of nourishment | Daily watering makes the plant grow too tall | Water given on a dry day evaporates at once
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the superstition whereby the breakage of a mirror occasions seven years of misfortune? | It is untrue; the sole hazard is the broken glass | It is true only where the mirror was exceedingly old | It is true unless the fragments are buried promptly | It is true, as broken glass draws misfortune to it
exam: Given that an old glass thermometer has been broken, ascertain by whom the resulting spill ought to be cleaned, and for what reason. | By an adult, since it may contain poisonous mercury | By the child, since the liquid is merely coloured water | By no one, since the liquid simply evaporates harmlessly | By the child, provided that thick rubber gloves are worn
